item.description:
@LaLiLuLeL0x (BasedToschi) joins @slop.atg.eth to unpack Glue — a Uniswap V4-based protocol he pitches as 'LPs gave tokens liquidity, Glue gives them solidity,' with backing, buyback-and-burn, staking rewards, and LP auto-compounding baked into a permissionless hook. He walks through GlueHook, the pot mechanic, and why he sees this as the backbone for revenue-sharing tokenomics on Ethereum L1.

They get deep into AI harnesses: both are Claude maxis, with Alessandro leaning on Opus for planning and Grok 4.6 as a cheaper second brain, plus a stint using Grok to get a Uniswap PR merged. He explains how AI transformed his auditing workflow across ~25 smart contracts built over a year, by standardizing attack surfaces and letting Claude constantly rewrite the test suite.

The show closes with @clawdbotatg.eth actually deploying a fresh CLAWD/ETH pool on Base with GlueHook attached — compounding, burning, and buying back live on-chain.

@slop.atg.eth hosts @w1nt3r_eth — co-creator of BasePaint, author of EVM From Scratch, and a longtime Solidity craftsman — for a conversation about what daily building looks like now that agents write most of the code. w1nt3r admits he barely opens an editor anymore, lives inside the Codex desktop app, and shipped things like tv.basepaint.xyz and cursor.exe.xyz almost entirely by prompting.

They get into BasePaint's 3-year anniversary and hackathon, the ai.txt skill file that let non-coders submit projects via Replit, and why the project stays lean with no VC, no token, and no distributions. w1nt3r reflects on losing the joy of reading smart contracts, the Outliers-style luck of being the right age for a technology wave, and why his own distribution has cratered even as his output has grown.

The episode closes on AI psychosis, the arbitrage gap between Twitter's AI bubble and the 8 billion people who've never used Claude, and w1nt3r's pitch: go do a magic trick for someone running their business on paper.

@shawmakesmagic (Shaw Walters, founder of Eliza Labs and creator of ElizaOS) joins @slop.atg.eth for a wide-ranging conversation about what he learned from the ai16z DAO run, the emotional cost of shipping in public during a token pump, and where he wants agent infrastructure to go next.

They dig into open-source agent frameworks, why closed harnesses frustrate him, and his current dev loop across three laptops with Codex and Claude. Shaw argues taste and alignment are the same problem, and that the real unlock for local AI isn't just privacy — it's personalization that a hyperscaler can never ship at scale.

The back half turns to collective ownership: crowdfunding DGX Sparks, DAOs as insurance layers, agent-mediated social feeds à la Tim Berners-Lee's Solid, Worldcoin as human-in-the-loop infrastructure, and why UBI risks a permanent underclass. A cameo from @clawdbotatg.eth closes things out.
